<?nvs
// The escalation ladder's reporting half, as a program: two records a
// program writes for itself, and the rung above an ordinary `catch`.
//
// [ADR 0020](/docs/decisions/0020.md) is the design and
// its load-bearing claim is about *sameness*: `Core\Log`'s JSON-Lines
// writer is the same native serialiser the engine floor calls directly, so
// the two can never disagree about log shape. Two writers that agree today
// is the failure this arrangement prevents, and it is the same reasoning
// goal `concurrency` used for one graph copy reached twice rather than two copies.
//
// `Core\Log::write` refuses a `secret` argument at the call site — a sink
// on ADR 0033's axis — while accepting `tainted` freely, because a field is
// *data* and logging untrusted input is the entire point (ADR 0088 § 7).
//
// The third line comes from the child, not from here. An uncaught throw
// climbs past every `catch` to the operator's handler script, which is
// named by that entry's own block in `nvs.toml`; the child's failure is a
// value on this side, exactly as in `examples/capability.nvs`, so this
// program still exits 0 having watched another one not.
//
// The three lines this prints are frozen by an exact check in a goal record
// under `data/goals/`, and each record names the line it was produced at
// (`rule:errors/a-record-names-where-it-was-produced`) — so moving a write
// below moves what that check expects.

Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Info, 'started');

// An ordinary failure, caught and reported. Nothing has escalated: this is
// the rung a program handles itself.
try {
    throw new RuntimeError('boom');
} catch (RuntimeError $failure) {
    Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Error, 'boom');
}

// And the rung above it, where the program no longer gets a say.
var $child = spawn script 'examples/logging/throws.nvs' with(
    output: 'inherit',
);
var $done = await $child;
